  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe CWA will be bereft of any interesting weather for the rest of \nthe work week. In the mid/upper-levels, the once-neutrally-tilting \ntrough has now become negatively-tilted while digging into the \nCanadian Maritimes. The synoptic cold front associated with this \ntrough has also crossed east of the spine of the Appalachian \nMountains while undergoing frontolysis, with a modest polar surface \nhigh, analyzed at 1024 mb, settling into the region. The cold, \ndense, sinking air has resulted in a crystal clear sky this early \nmorning, with river valley fog continuing to rapidly spread across \nthe mountains and lowlands. High-resolution NWP guidance has \nstruggled to initialize any fog, so the grids were manually tweaked \nto account for the trend in observations. Fog will dissipate at the \nusual time after sunrise, or by about 14Z, for all locales. Frost \ncontinues to be expected across the northeastern mountains of WV, \nand the Frost Advisory remains in effect through 13Z for Barbour, \nPocahontas, and Randolph Counties. Morning lows are forecast to \nbottom out near 33 degrees across those areas. \n\nDespite this airmass being of polar origin, it has already modified \ndue to the weakened state of the prior cold front that passed \nthrough the region yesterday morning and the respective displacement \nof the mean troughing to the north of the CWA. The airmass will \ncontinue to modify over the next few days as the restoration of \nreturn flow occurs ahead of another shortwave trough digging \nsoutheastward towards the Great Lakes region. A warm-up will follow \nthrough the middle of the week before another weak (and dry) cold \nfront moves through the region Thursday, knocking temperatures down \na few degrees. PoPs remain NIL across the region through Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe next potential for rain is forecast to arrive Sunday and into \nearly next week. Global NWP guidance continues to indicate that the \nremnants of Invest 92L, currently located in the Bay of Campeche, \nwill become absorbed into the mean troughing across the Lower 48 as \nit moves poleward. However, discrepancies exist on the track of this \nundeveloped tropical system by the time it moves inland. Therefore, \nit is too soon to determine rainfall amounts, duration, and extent; \nand impacts, if any. Regardless, at least some rain is forecast to \narrive Sunday and beyond, which will put an end to the week long dry \nspell across the region.",
